-- title:
--   Nagata's criterion for a single prime element
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring which is an integral domain and Noetherian, and let $x \in R$ be a prime element, i.e. $x$ is non-zero, not a unit, and $x \mid ab$ implies $x \mid a$ or $x \mid b$. Let $S$ be a commutative ring which is an integral domain, equipped with an $R$-algebra structure making it a localisation of $R$ away from $x$ (that is, a localisation of $R$ at the multiplicative submonoid of powers of $x$, so that $S$ realises $R[1/x]$ up to isomorphism). Assume that $S$ is a unique factorisation monoid. The conclusion is that $R$ itself is a unique factorisation monoid: every non-zero non-unit of $R$ factors into irreducibles, uniquely up to order and associates. Only the single prime element $x$ is inverted; the statement is not the general form of the criterion in which one localises at a multiplicative set generated by a family of prime elements.
--
--   This is Nagata's criterion (factoriality descends from $R[1/x]$ to $R$ when $x$ is prime), in the special case of inverting one prime element. It serves as the inductive step towards the factoriality of regular local rings, and is cited in the proof of [`IsRegularRing.uniqueFactorizationMonoid_of_isLocalRing`](thm.html#IsRegularRing.uniqueFactorizationMonoid_of_isLocalRing).
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_UniqueFactorizationMonoid_of_isLocalization_away_of_prime.lean

import Mathlib

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
